WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.789
Day two is run by the people who touch the
estate most often and decide the least,

2
00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:08.799
so every decision sits with someone
who isn't holding the keyboard,

3
00:00:08.799 --> 00:00:11.788
and every artifact has one
owner and a review date.

4
00:00:11.788 --> 00:00:13.925
The service desk has three lines.

5
00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:16.910
The first takes every
request, classifies it,

6
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.025
verifies the person and executes
against an approved ticket.

7
00:00:21.025 --> 00:00:24.837
Cloud services is the escalation
tier, not a second queue.

8
00:00:24.837 --> 00:00:26.437
Technicians approve nothing.

9
00:00:26.437 --> 00:00:30.913
The supervisor approves routine
credential work for ordinary users,

10
00:00:30.913 --> 00:00:32.688
never a ticket they execute.

11
00:00:32.688 --> 00:00:36.713
Platform owners own the runbooks
and what each catalog item does.

12
00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:40.733
The control owner, the CISO or
the identity governance officer,

13
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.175
owns the approval gate.

14
00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:45.825
The service management team
builds both and decides neither:

15
00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:50.763
changing an approval rule is a normal change
the change advisory board authorizes.

16
00:00:50.763 --> 00:00:54.750
The configuration database owner
keeps the model, not the facts.

17
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.118
The board has security and privacy
members and authorizes changes,

18
00:00:59.118 --> 00:01:03.875
but doesn't accept risk; that goes to
the CISO or the authorizing official.

19
00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:07.188
A contracted desk fits,
because contractors prepare

20
00:01:07.188 --> 00:01:10.250
and execute while government
officials decide.
